The triangle $ABC$ has vertices $A(0,0)$, $B(6,0)$ and $C(3,4)$.
Using the side lengths, what type of triangle is $\triangle ABC$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-1,-1) grid (7,5);
\draw[<->] (-1.5,0)--(7.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-1.5)--(0,5.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(6,0)--(3,4)--cycle;
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\footnotesize] at (0,0) {$A$};
\fill (6,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below right,font=\footnotesize] at (6,0) {$B$};
\fill (3,4) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (3,4) {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The triangle $PQR$ has vertices $P(-1,-2)$, $Q(1,2)$ and $R(5,0)$.
It is right-angled.
Using the gradients of the sides, at which vertex is the right angle?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-2,-3) grid (6,3);
\draw[<->] (-2.5,0)--(6.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.5)--(0,3.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (-1,-2)--(1,2)--(5,0)--cycle;
\fill (-1,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\footnotesize] at (-1,-2) {$P$};
\fill (1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left,font=\footnotesize] at (1,2) {$Q$};
\fill (5,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (5,0) {$R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The triangle $ABC$ has vertices $A(-2,-1)$, $B(1,-2)$ and $C(3,4)$.
Using the side lengths and Pythagoras' theorem, what type of triangle is $\triangle ABC$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (4,5);
\draw[<->] (-3.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.5)--(0,5.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (-2,-1)--(1,-2)--(3,4)--cycle;
\fill (-2,-1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-2,-1) {$A$};
\fill (1,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (1,-2) {$B$};
\fill (3,4) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above right,font=\footnotesize] at (3,4) {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Find the perimeter of the triangle $ABC$ with vertices $A(1,1)$, $B(5,4)$ and $C(1,4)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,5);
\draw[<->] (-0.5,0)--(6.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.5)--(0,5.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (1,1)--(5,4)--(1,4)--cycle;
\fill (1,1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left,font=\footnotesize] at (1,1) {$A$};
\fill (5,4) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (5,4) {$B$};
\fill (1,4) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left,font=\footnotesize] at (1,4) {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For the quadrilateral $EFGH$, the diagonals $EG$ and $FH$ have the same midpoint.
What does this show?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (4,4);
\draw[<->] (-3.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.5)--(0,4.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (-2,-1)--(2,-2)--(3,2)--(-1,3)--cycle;
\fill (-2,-1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-2,-1) {$E$};
\fill (2,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (2,-2) {$F$};
\fill (3,2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (3,2) {$G$};
\fill (-1,3) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (-1,3) {$H$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The quadrilateral $ABCD$ has vertices $A(-1,-3)$, $B(2,1)$, $C(-1,5)$ and $D(-4,1)$, and all four sides have length $5$.
What is the most precise name for $ABCD$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-5,-4) grid (3,6);
\draw[<->] (-5.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-4.5)--(0,6.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (-1,-3)--(2,1)--(-1,5)--(-4,1)--cycle;
\fill (-1,-3) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (-1,-3) {$A$};
\fill (2,1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (2,1) {$B$};
\fill (-1,5) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (-1,5) {$C$};
\fill (-4,1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-4,1) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The quadrilateral $ABCD$ has vertices $A(-3,-2)$, $B(1,-3)$, $C(3,1)$ and $D(-1,2)$.
Both pairs of opposite sides have equal gradients, and no angle is a right angle.
What is the most precise name for $ABCD$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-4,-4) grid (4,3);
\draw[<->] (-4.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-4.5)--(0,3.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (-3,-2)--(1,-3)--(3,1)--(-1,2)--cycle;
\fill (-3,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-3,-2) {$A$};
\fill (1,-3) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (1,-3) {$B$};
\fill (3,1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (3,1) {$C$};
\fill (-1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (-1,2) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$ABCD$ is a parallelogram with vertices $A(-2,1)$, $B(2,-1)$ and $C(5,3)$, listed in order.
Find the coordinates of the fourth vertex $D$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-3,-2) grid (6,6);
\draw[<->] (-3.5,0)--(6.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-2.5)--(0,6.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (-2,1)--(2,-1)--(5,3);
\fill (-2,1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-2,1) {$A$};
\fill (2,-1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (2,-1) {$B$};
\fill (5,3) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (5,3) {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The quadrilateral $ABCD$ has vertices $A(2,1)$, $B(-1,2)$, $C(-2,-1)$ and $D(1,-2)$.
Its four sides are all equal in length and its two diagonals are also equal in length.
What is the most precise name for $ABCD$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[<->] (-3.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.5)--(0,3.5) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (2,1)--(-1,2)--(-2,-1)--(1,-2)--cycle;
\fill (2,1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (2,1) {$A$};
\fill (-1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (-1,2) {$B$};
\fill (-2,-1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-2,-1) {$C$};
\fill (1,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (1,-2) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
